From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: opi5 <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] rknpu: power off once the IOMMU has suspended

rknpu_power_off() waits for the NPU's IOMMU to runtime-suspend before it
gates the NPU clocks, because the IOMMU's suspend writes every MMU bank.
The compat stub for rockchip_iommu_is_enabled() returned "the device has
an IOMMU group", which is always true. So every power-off timed out after
20 ms, logged "iommu still enabled" and took its power reference back:
the NPU was never clock-gated, and the delayed power-off retried every
3 s for as long as the module was loaded. Module removal and system
suspend failed the same way.

Look up the IOMMU's device and report whether it is runtime-active. Its
idle check is only requested through the device link, on the freezable
pm_wq, so run it synchronously first.
---
 .../compat/soc/rockchip/rockchip_iommu.h      | 43 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 36 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/rknpu/src/include/compat/soc/rockchip/rockchip_iommu.h b/drivers/rknpu/src/include/compat/soc/rockchip/rockchip_iommu.h
index 75510929c68c..e2154d8388ae 100644
--- a/drivers/rknpu/src/include/compat/soc/rockchip/rockchip_iommu.h
+++ b/drivers/rknpu/src/include/compat/soc/rockchip/rockchip_iommu.h
@@ -6,16 +6,45 @@
 
 #include <linux/device.h>
 #include <linux/iommu.h>
+#include <linux/platform_device.h>
+#include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 
+/* The IOMMU that translates for @dev, with a reference, or NULL. */
+static inline struct device *rknpu_compat_iommu_dev(struct device *dev)
+{
+	struct iommu_fwspec *fwspec = dev_iommu_fwspec_get(dev);
+
+	if (!fwspec || !fwspec->iommu_fwnode)
+		return NULL;
+	return bus_find_device_by_fwnode(&platform_bus_type, fwspec->iommu_fwnode);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Whether @dev's IOMMU is still runtime-active.  rknpu_power_off() waits for
+ * it to suspend before it gates the NPU's clocks: the IOMMU's runtime suspend
+ * writes every MMU bank, and the core1/core2 banks run on those clocks.
+ *
+ * opi5: this stub returned "@dev has an IOMMU group", which is always true,
+ * so every power-off timed out after 20 ms, logged "iommu still enabled" and
+ * took its power reference back.  The NPU was never clock-gated, and the
+ * delayed power-off retried every 3 s for as long as the module was loaded.
+ *
+ * The PM_RUNTIME device link only requests the IOMMU's idle check, on the
+ * freezable pm_wq, which does not run during system suspend.  Run it here,
+ * synchronously; it suspends the IOMMU once @dev has released it.
+ */
 static inline bool rockchip_iommu_is_enabled(struct device *dev)
 {
-	/* If the device has an IOMMU group, IOMMU is enabled */
-	struct iommu_group *group = iommu_group_get(dev);
-	if (group) {
-		iommu_group_put(group);
-		return true;
-	}
-	return false;
+	struct device *iommu_dev = rknpu_compat_iommu_dev(dev);
+	bool enabled;
+
+	if (!iommu_dev)
+		return false;
+	if (!pm_runtime_status_suspended(iommu_dev))
+		pm_runtime_idle(iommu_dev);
+	enabled = !pm_runtime_status_suspended(iommu_dev);
+	put_device(iommu_dev);
+	return enabled;
 }
 
 static inline void rockchip_iommu_disable(struct device *dev)
